## Scope and search (claude-forms)

Deliver what was asked, at the scope intended. Don't add features, refactors, helpers, or abstractions beyond the task. Simplest thing that works — few files are not enough if the code is brittle or unreadable. Extra robustness and security hardening only when requested.

Before new files or utilities: Grep/Glob/Read the repo; extend existing code when it fits. Don't reinvent.

When extending a pattern, match the nearest live sibling. If the draft would exceed it, stop and ask before writing more.

Prefer dumb boring flat code over style theater. Write so a reader with little programming experience can follow the order of operations. Use descriptive names, braces, and explicit `if` statements, loops, and `async`/`await` when clearer. Simple ternaries or collection methods are fine for one obvious choice or transformation; do not nest or chain them to compress several steps. Use `async function main()` for hook/CLI entrypoints; `for` loops for multi-step transforms; one shared copy for duplicate install/uninstall logic; full-word names in tests. Prefer rewriting a local unit over stacking patches that leave dead paths. Handle edges you own; keep types honest (no cast stacks); test non-trivial branching you add; keep long instructional prose out of hot code paths. Make architecture obvious (file-top note, marked entrypoint, fewer helpers, comment non-obvious regex/magic). Delete one-off scripts you created. Update docs your change makes wrong. Always format touched files; on new JS/TS repos add minimal prettier + `format` script if missing.

Default: do the work yourself. Do not use subagents to verify your own work. Spawn agents only for large independent parallel tracks or when explicitly asked.

Lead with the outcome. Only claim progress you can evidence with tool results from this session. Final user message: plain language for a reader who saw none of the work.

Effort is a host/API setting (`low`/`medium`/`high`/`xhigh`/`max`): prefer lower effort on routine work; high effort can increase unrequested tidying on Fable.

Optional: the always-on `no-overengineer` rule is installed by `install:user`. For model-specific behavior, invoke skills `prompt-general`, `prompt-opus-5`, or `prompt-fable-5`.
